WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.398 --> 00:00:03.159
- On this episode I talk
about turning points,

00:00:03.159 --> 00:00:07.154
content, real estate, and chugging.

00:00:07.154 --> 00:00:09.254
(upbeat music)
You ask questions,

00:00:09.254 --> 00:00:11.089
and I answer them.

00:00:11.089 --> 00:00:14.837
This is the #AskGaryVee Show.

00:00:14.837 --> 00:00:17.207
(upbeat music)

00:00:17.207 --> 00:00:19.692
Hey everybody, this is Gary Vay-ner-chuk,

00:00:19.692 --> 00:00:23.536
and welcome to Episode 36
of the #AskGaryVee Show.

00:00:23.536 --> 00:00:25.531
Fun fact about the number 36:

00:00:25.531 --> 00:00:28.561
it's the last time I'm doing
a fun fact about a number.

00:00:28.561 --> 00:00:32.497
- [Voiceover] Barry asks:
"Gary, love to hear your opinion

00:00:32.497 --> 00:00:35.074
on whether a PR company
or a person should be

00:00:35.074 --> 00:00:37.895
overseeing social media for a business."

00:00:37.895 --> 00:00:41.135
- Barry, great question,
and before I even answer it,

00:00:41.135 --> 00:00:43.514
I just want to thank you
for being a long time

00:00:43.514 --> 00:00:45.871
interactor friend with me.

00:00:45.871 --> 00:00:47.599
I don't like using the word fan,

00:00:47.599 --> 00:00:49.318
but I say it sometimes, it just slips.

00:00:49.318 --> 00:00:50.909
Barry, I've always
enjoyed our conversations

00:00:50.909 --> 00:00:52.593
on Twitter over the last four years,

00:00:52.593 --> 00:00:54.461
so thrilled to have you on the show.

00:00:54.461 --> 00:00:56.736
I think I know where you're
going with this question,

00:00:56.736 --> 00:00:59.581
which is the notion of,
VaynerMedia was built

00:00:59.581 --> 00:01:02.007
on coming into companies and taking the

00:01:02.007 --> 00:01:04.271
social media away from PR companies.

00:01:04.271 --> 00:01:06.048
Some of the bigger PR companies
in the world right now

00:01:06.048 --> 00:01:08.230
have built out social media departments

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:10.946
and they've done a nice
job, to varying degrees.

00:01:10.946 --> 00:01:13.268
I wanted to answer this question because

00:01:13.268 --> 00:01:14.881
I want to get people around the psychology

00:01:14.881 --> 00:01:17.888
of the difference between PR and social,

00:01:17.888 --> 00:01:21.047
and why I do think that,
of course there's people,

00:01:21.047 --> 00:01:24.388
hundreds of people in
this company have worked

00:01:24.388 --> 00:01:27.721
in PR before, so there's
some great things about PR.

00:01:27.721 --> 00:01:30.402
Being able to handle
pressure, the speed of it.

00:01:30.402 --> 00:01:33.629
The difference though, is PR is very B2B.

00:01:33.629 --> 00:01:36.184
When you're a PR person
and you're working with

00:01:36.184 --> 00:01:39.505
a client, the Yankees,
you're trying to get them

00:01:39.505 --> 00:01:43.300
press in the New York Times,
The New York Post, ESPN.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:45.483
You're working directly with a human being

00:01:45.483 --> 00:01:47.585
who's the gatekeeper to make a decision.

00:01:47.585 --> 00:01:49.081
When you're doing social, you're dealing

00:01:49.081 --> 00:01:51.740
with all the fans, and it's much more B2C.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:54.863
So I don't have a problem if a PR person

00:01:54.863 --> 00:01:58.798
or company is doing the social
for a company or individual,

00:01:58.798 --> 00:02:01.398
I just want to make sure
they have a different gear

00:02:01.398 --> 00:02:03.197
in their brand understanding,

00:02:03.197 --> 00:02:05.915
brain, not brand, but that
was an interesting slip-up.

00:02:05.915 --> 00:02:08.537
I just want to make
sure they have that gear

00:02:08.537 --> 00:02:10.779
to know that they need
to be looking at this

00:02:10.779 --> 00:02:14.412
as a B2C game, versus
the B2B game that is PR.

00:02:14.412 --> 00:02:16.955
- [Voiceover] eastcountytoday says,

00:02:16.955 --> 00:02:19.078
"Gary, love the iTunes touch.

00:02:19.078 --> 00:02:20.774
When was the moment you knew you would be

00:02:20.774 --> 00:02:23.154
okay when starting your company?"

00:02:23.154 --> 00:02:26.497
- East County, right?

00:02:26.497 --> 00:02:30.200
That's a little bone thugs reference to

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:32.673
episode, I can't remember.

00:02:32.673 --> 00:02:36.097
East county, the moment I
knew that I was gonna make it

00:02:36.097 --> 00:02:40.300
was the first day I walked
into my dad's liquor store.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:43.260
And the reason I decided
to answer this question

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:45.316
and trying to find value
for everybody watching

00:02:45.316 --> 00:02:46.872
other than me bragging about that I

00:02:46.872 --> 00:02:49.193
had the bravado from day one was,

00:02:49.193 --> 00:02:53.732
the notion of not even
worrying about that moment.

00:02:53.732 --> 00:02:56.611
Meaning, one of the biggest
things that I'm trying to teach,

00:02:56.611 --> 00:02:58.237
I'm turning a lot today.

00:02:58.237 --> 00:03:00.256
One of the things I'm trying
to teach all my management

00:03:00.256 --> 00:03:04.156
here at VaynerMedia, and all
my founders in my start-up

00:03:04.156 --> 00:03:06.699
investments and my
co-founders in companies

00:03:06.699 --> 00:03:09.869
that had meeting with my
co-founders at Resy last night,

00:03:09.869 --> 00:03:12.829
the number one thing I
keep telling everybody

00:03:12.829 --> 00:03:15.580
is to not worry about the
things that don't matter.

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:17.519
Worrying about or trying to figure out,

00:03:17.519 --> 00:03:19.365
this is the moment when I made it,

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:21.245
is something that I think cripples people,

00:03:21.245 --> 00:03:23.300
and I just don't even
think about those things.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:25.586
I could answer this question two ways,

00:03:25.586 --> 00:03:27.503
which are the two right
ways, which is one:

00:03:27.503 --> 00:03:29.139
The moment I walked into my dad's store,

00:03:29.139 --> 00:03:30.845
because I had that
confidence, or I could answer

00:03:30.845 --> 00:03:32.902
the other way that's equally as true,

00:03:32.902 --> 00:03:34.607
pulling on both sides
like a bridge, which is,

00:03:34.607 --> 00:03:36.441
I haven't made it yet.

00:03:36.441 --> 00:03:39.518
They both are right, and the
truth is, outside of this

00:03:39.518 --> 00:03:44.359
question, I don't think about
it at all, ever, period.

00:03:44.359 --> 00:03:47.435
And the reason I'm answering
the question is because

00:03:47.435 --> 00:03:49.676
I'm trying to get as many
of you who are watching

00:03:49.676 --> 00:03:52.578
the show right now to not
worry about those things.

00:03:52.578 --> 00:03:55.422
Worry about executing,
worry about feeling good

00:03:55.422 --> 00:03:58.441
about your life, don't
worry about making it.

00:03:58.441 --> 00:04:00.518
Because making it is an outside force.

00:04:00.518 --> 00:04:02.701
The inside force of you just doing it

00:04:02.701 --> 00:04:05.022
is what you should be focused on.

00:04:05.022 --> 00:04:07.137
- [Voiceover] Kyle asks,

00:04:07.137 --> 00:04:09.458
"Gary, is there a way to
drive traffic to a website

00:04:09.458 --> 00:04:13.008
when posting content directly to Facebook?"

00:04:13.008 --> 00:04:15.320
- Kyle, yes.

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:17.536
(bell ringing)

00:04:19.867 --> 00:04:23.760
As you can tell, that
Facebook post on my fan page

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:27.219
drove a crap load of traffic
to my Medium article,

00:04:27.219 --> 00:04:30.250
which is content.

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:33.047
I answered this question
because I wanted to show you

00:04:33.047 --> 00:04:35.612
raw details because I
think raw details is even

00:04:35.612 --> 00:04:38.839
a deeper version of this
show, and I continue

00:04:38.839 --> 00:04:43.839
to try to go deep within myself
to really drive you value,

00:04:44.599 --> 00:04:47.837
especially because this is
only a 50 episode experiment.

00:04:47.837 --> 00:04:49.416
Just kidding.

00:04:49.416 --> 00:04:51.970
And so, the answer is absolutely.

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:53.653
Facebook is actually
probably one of the biggest

00:04:53.653 --> 00:04:57.194
drivers of content
awareness outside of itself

00:04:57.194 --> 00:05:00.676
to other destinations
in the world right now,

00:05:00.676 --> 00:05:03.288
so the question is, how
do you do it organically,

00:05:03.288 --> 00:05:06.028
how do you do it in a paid, targeted way?

00:05:06.028 --> 00:05:07.549
What I just showed you was organic.

00:05:07.549 --> 00:05:10.869
I have a pretty big foundation
of 150,000 fans on that page,

00:05:10.869 --> 00:05:12.795
but there's people that I've
seen post content that have

00:05:12.795 --> 00:05:16.012
800 fans, and enough people
shared it and enough people

00:05:16.012 --> 00:05:19.448
liked it, enough people commented
it and shared it not only

00:05:19.448 --> 00:05:22.513
within Facebook but outside
of it, that it created fire.

00:05:22.513 --> 00:05:26.414
Facebook is content
awareness infrastructure

00:05:26.414 --> 00:05:28.166
in a 2015 world.

00:05:28.166 --> 00:05:31.092
So not only is there a way, I
think it's one of the singular

00:05:31.092 --> 00:05:34.691
best ways, and so I would
highly recommend making

00:05:34.691 --> 00:05:37.105
an investment in Facebook
fan pages, recognizing

00:05:37.105 --> 00:05:40.553
the distribution
opportunities that it creates

00:05:40.553 --> 00:05:43.753
for content you're putting
outside of its network.

00:05:43.753 --> 00:05:45.742
- Hey Gary, I'm a realtor, and our team

00:05:45.742 --> 00:05:47.983
puts out a lot of video.

00:05:47.983 --> 00:05:50.061
(bell ringing)
- Ha, the ding.

00:05:50.061 --> 00:05:52.019
- In Episode Eight you
said it was important

00:05:52.019 --> 00:05:54.160
to put up daily content,
so my question to you is,

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:56.016
if you were a realtor, what kind

00:05:56.016 --> 00:05:59.092
of daily video content would you produce?

00:05:59.092 --> 00:06:02.484
- So that was a tremendous video.

00:06:02.484 --> 00:06:04.886
Let's all at the VaynerNation
pay attention to multiple

00:06:04.886 --> 00:06:07.486
things, including he was wearing
the R.O.I. of your mother

00:06:07.486 --> 00:06:11.122
T-shirt, the fact that
he dinged the jab-jab-jab

00:06:11.122 --> 00:06:13.130
in the background, a random man walked by

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:14.443
in the background, which is a reference

00:06:14.443 --> 00:06:16.207
to some of the stuff
we've done on the show.

00:06:16.207 --> 00:06:18.250
If you're listening on the
podcast, I highly recommend

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:19.806
you go to YouTube and watch this episode

00:06:19.806 --> 00:06:22.580
just to watch this video,
'cause it was tremendous.

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:24.542
My answer is very simple.

00:06:24.542 --> 00:06:26.609
If I was a realtor, the
thing that I would do more

00:06:26.609 --> 00:06:29.847
than anything is actually review the area

00:06:29.847 --> 00:06:32.622
around the places where I sell homes.

00:06:32.622 --> 00:06:33.841
Let me explain.

00:06:33.841 --> 00:06:35.780
If I'm selling homes in
Millburn, New Jersey,

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:37.750
I'm putting out a daily
piece of content reviewing

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:41.326
the school, then I'm interviewing
the individual teachers

00:06:41.326 --> 00:06:43.334
if I can get access to
them, then I'm reviewing

00:06:43.334 --> 00:06:46.433
every local business, the
Subway shop, the wine shop,

00:06:46.433 --> 00:06:49.393
then I'm interviewing
literally people that have

00:06:49.393 --> 00:06:51.101
lived in the neighborhood for 50 years.

00:06:51.101 --> 00:06:53.785
I'm putting out content to
make you romantic around the

00:06:53.785 --> 00:06:57.650
stories in the area, because
people pick them for utility.

00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:01.203
What I mean by that is,
convenience of transportation,

00:07:01.203 --> 00:07:03.129
how quickly from the
office, but they also pick

00:07:03.129 --> 00:07:05.370
because of the school
systems, and there's a lot

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:07.286
of data out there on that, but how about

00:07:07.286 --> 00:07:08.969
making it a little warmer and interviewing

00:07:08.969 --> 00:07:10.780
Miss Robinson the third grade teacher,.

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:14.171
And then obviously kind of
the amenities around it,

00:07:14.171 --> 00:07:17.120
the playground, the best stores.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:18.848
I remember a realtor
telling me that people moved

00:07:18.848 --> 00:07:21.240
to Short Hills because of Wine Library.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:22.703
I thought that was cool.

00:07:22.703 --> 00:07:25.048
It felt like such an anchor to that area.

00:07:25.048 --> 00:07:27.439
And so what I would do is daily content

00:07:27.439 --> 00:07:29.983
on the 20 mile radius or 10 mile radius

00:07:29.983 --> 00:07:32.582
around the area where you sell the homes.

00:07:32.582 --> 00:07:34.661
The stories that are tucked
away in the businesses

00:07:34.661 --> 00:07:37.667
and the school system,
and the iconic neighbors

00:07:37.667 --> 00:07:39.142
that have been around forever,

00:07:39.142 --> 00:07:41.719
those stories are the narrative
that will create emotion

00:07:41.719 --> 00:07:45.144
which will be on a tipping
point scale, on a 50/50,

00:07:45.144 --> 00:07:48.348
may be the thing that tips
someone to buying your home.

00:07:48.348 --> 00:07:50.808
- [Voiceover] Sean asks, "Gary,

00:07:50.808 --> 00:07:52.504
most big successes have a huge

00:07:52.504 --> 00:07:54.849
turning point where
things really take off.

00:07:54.849 --> 00:07:57.566
What was that turning
point for Wine Library?"

00:07:57.566 --> 00:07:59.725
- Sean, great question.

00:07:59.725 --> 00:08:02.627
I guess there were some turning points

00:08:02.627 --> 00:08:05.227
when the Wine Spectator ad that we ran,

00:08:05.227 --> 00:08:07.072
the first New York Times full page ad,

00:08:07.072 --> 00:08:09.407
the time I reset the score and took 50%

00:08:09.407 --> 00:08:12.390
of the beer off the floor
and added more wine,

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:15.362
when I started WineLibrary.com,

00:08:15.362 --> 00:08:17.185
the day I started the email service,

00:08:17.185 --> 00:08:19.889
the day I jumped into Robert
Parker's forums in '97

00:08:19.889 --> 00:08:22.688
and became part of the
internet community around wine,

00:08:22.688 --> 00:08:26.809
the 2000 Bordeaux Vintage,
when we bought heavy,

00:08:26.809 --> 00:08:29.618
when I first started
promoting wines nobody ever

00:08:29.618 --> 00:08:32.811
heard of on email, Richard
Partridge Cabernet comes to mind,

00:08:32.811 --> 00:08:35.015
when I hired Brandon.

00:08:35.015 --> 00:08:38.232
As you can tell, there are
many moments that we made it,

00:08:38.232 --> 00:08:40.275
but it was just trucking
along, building on top

00:08:40.275 --> 00:08:42.409
of each other step by step.

00:08:42.409 --> 00:08:45.614
My friends, if you listen to
two of my answers on this show,

00:08:45.614 --> 00:08:48.227
you understand one very
interesting thing about me,

00:08:48.227 --> 00:08:51.989
which is, I may have
the energy of the hare,

00:08:51.989 --> 00:08:54.427
but I am the tortoise.
(bell ringing)

00:08:54.427 --> 00:08:55.773
You know what I'm putting up there, right?

00:08:55.773 --> 00:08:56.863
That beautiful thing you did, Zak.

00:08:56.863 --> 00:08:57.793
Show Zak.

00:08:57.793 --> 00:08:59.325
You did a very nice job on that one.

00:08:59.325 --> 00:09:02.436
For everybody listening,
I'm pointing to the tortoise

00:09:02.436 --> 00:09:04.096
and hare image I put out on Instagram.

00:09:04.096 --> 00:09:06.487
Go check me out on Instagram/garyvee.

00:09:06.487 --> 00:09:11.487
Anyway, when I made it,
the turning point moment,

00:09:12.862 --> 00:09:15.880
everybody who's watching
and asking these questions

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:18.979
are looking for this
sign, like I saw the sign,

00:09:18.979 --> 00:09:21.882
It's not that.

00:09:21.882 --> 00:09:25.422
It's head down, you love
and believe in what you do,

00:09:25.422 --> 00:09:27.779
and you just never think
about those moments,

00:09:27.779 --> 00:09:29.637
you just keep trucking along.

00:09:29.637 --> 00:09:32.143
It's lunch pail mentality, it's old school

00:09:32.143 --> 00:09:35.302
Eastern European
put-in-the-work mentality.

00:09:35.302 --> 00:09:37.343
I don't think about these things, guys.

00:09:37.343 --> 00:09:38.981
The Fortune 40 Under
40 that just happened,

00:09:38.981 --> 00:09:40.467
is that a turning point in my career?

00:09:40.467 --> 00:09:42.499
Sure, some people now
think of me differently

00:09:42.499 --> 00:09:44.948
'cause I'm in the context of
those people, but it's not.

00:09:44.948 --> 00:09:48.198
It's just chug and chug and chug and chug

00:09:48.198 --> 00:09:51.506
and chug and chug and chug and chug,

00:09:51.506 --> 00:09:53.609
And so chug.

00:09:53.609 --> 00:09:55.083
Thanks for watching the show.

00:09:55.083 --> 00:09:56.326
You like that DRock?
(giggling off camera)

00:09:56.326 --> 00:09:58.160
Don't edit that part, I
want your giggle in there.

00:09:58.160 --> 00:09:59.807
Thanks for watching the show everybody.

00:09:59.807 --> 00:10:01.643
Episode 36 in the bag.

00:10:01.643 --> 00:10:04.824
Question of the day.
(sighs)

00:10:04.824 --> 00:10:08.224
You know, to use this
whole theme of the show,

00:10:08.224 --> 00:10:10.326
question of the day, very simply,

00:10:10.326 --> 00:10:12.380
do you think you're a chugger,

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:14.725
or do you think that you
had a turning point moment,

00:10:14.725 --> 00:10:17.686
and if it was, what was that?

00:10:17.686 --> 00:10:20.240
And by the way, Episode 35, we dropped off

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:23.201
